# MizanZarb

> The scale for your money.

A local-first, open-source money operating system for solo software
developers. Know your MRR, burn, runway, and break-even — without a
cloud account, without vendor lock-in, without lying to yourself about
the numbers.

---

## The Problem

Solo software developers — indie hackers, SaaS founders, app makers —
run real businesses. They collect revenue from Stripe, Paddle, Gumroad,
the App Store, and direct sales. They pay for infrastructure that scales
with users (Vercel, Supabase, OpenAI, S3) and tools that don't (Figma,
GitHub, Linear). They owe quarterly taxes with no payroll withholding.

Their existing tools fall into two useless buckets:

1. **Accounting tools** (FreshBooks, Wave, QuickBooks) — know what you
   spent, blind to what your business is worth. Built for invoice-heavy
   service freelancers, not product-based SaaS.

2. **Revenue trackers** (Solopreneur, qdBox, SaaSMon) — know what you
   earned, blind to what it cost, and whether you're actually profitable.

Nobody combines revenue normalization, cost separation, survival math,
and unit economics in one tool. Nobody does it local-first. Nobody does
it open-source. Nobody gives you a scriptable CLI.

**MizanZarb fills that gap.**

---

## Who It's For

A solo developer running one or more software products, collecting
revenue from at least one subscription platform, paying for
infrastructure that scales with users, and filing their own taxes.

They are technical. They live in a terminal. They distrust cloud
services with their financial data. They want their numbers to be
**correct**, not pretty. They want to script things. They want their
data in a file they can read, grep, back up, and take with them.

They are not looking for accounting software. They are looking for a
**business instrument**.

