## What It Does

### 1. Revenue normalization
Turns monthly, annual, lifetime, and one-time revenue into one honest
MRR number. Annual deals divided by 12. Lifetime deals amortized over
a configurable window. Multi-currency with historical FX rates. No
platform is special-cased; every source flows through the same pipeline.

### 2. Real gross margin
Every expense is tagged COGS or fixed. Vercel scales with users, so
it's COGS. Figma doesn't, so it's fixed. This distinction — which no
competitor makes — is what produces a real gross margin instead of a
meaningless "profit" number.

### 3. Survival metrics
Runway = available cash ÷ trailing-3-month net burn. Break-even MRR =
monthly fixed costs ÷ gross margin. Days-to-zero. What-if scenarios
("what if churn doubles?"). Alerts when cash is running low.

### 4. Unit economics
ARPU, LTV (simple and real), CAC, LTV:CAC ratio, payback period,
revenue churn, customer churn, cohort retention, resurrection rate.
The metrics that decide whether the business is alive.

### 5. Product-level truth
Dev hours logged per product. Net revenue per product. Effective
hourly rate per product. Comparison to your consulting rate. When a
product pays $12/hr and your consulting rate is $75/hr, that's not a
minor detail — it's a career decision, and the tool tells you.

### 6. Tax reality
Country-aware tax reserve. Quarterly estimate. Locked reserve balance
that never counts as spendable cash. Deductible flag per expense.
Export in a format your accountant accepts. VAT/sales tax handling
for EU/UK customers.

### 7. Customer concentration risk
Top-1, top-3, top-10 customer share of MRR. Benchmarked as healthy,
watch, or dangerous. Alerts when a single customer is a business risk.

### 8. Local-first, scriptable, zero lock-in
Your data is a JSON file. Everything works offline. Every report
exports to CSV or JSON. Full CLI for scripting:
`mizan report pnl --month 2026-10 | mail accountant@example.com`.
Backup and restore commands. Git-friendly state.

